  "rationale": "Direct-match candidates for the target EFO term “ovarian neoplasm” are present (multiple models with trait_reported/phenotyping_reported “Malignant neoplasm of ovary”, including PGS000544–PGS000564). Among these exact-label matches, PGS000549 has the strongest reported discrimination in its peer set (full-model AUROC 0.567 with EUR evaluation; also the highest/near-highest Nagelkerke pseudo-R² shown, 0.00996, and per-SD OR 1.263 [1.15–1.387]) while maintaining the same basic covariate pattern (age, sex, batch, PCs 1–4) as the other PRSweb ovarian-neoplasm models, making it the best-supported option within the exact ovarian-neoplasm endpoint family available in the context. Limitation: the evidence is still sub-optimal because reported performance is largely full-model AUROC (no PRS-only AUROC/R²), several near-clone PRSweb models have very similar metadata and metrics (e.g., PGS000550 AUROC 0.568; PGS000551 AUROC 0.558), and higher-AUROC ovarian-carcinoma models exist but are not exact “ovarian neoplasm” label matches, reducing certainty that this is unequivocally the best overall direct-match for a broad ovarian neoplasm target."
}

{"outcome":"DIRECT_HIGH_QUALITY","best_model_id":"PGS000541","confidence":"Moderate","rationale":"Direct-match candidates include PGS000541 and PGS000542 (trait_efo = uterine cancer; phenotyping_reported = malignant neoplasm of uterus) and PGS001795 (trait_reported uterine cancer, but trait_efo = uterine carcinoma). Endometrial cancer / endometrial carcinoma scores are also plausible uterine-cancer umbrella matches per domain knowledge, but they are not exact-label uterine cancer endpoints.

Among the exact uterine-cancer endpoint models, PGS000541 is preferred over PGS000542 because it shows slightly better discrimination in the same publication family with essentially identical endpoint, ancestry (EUR), covariates (age/sex/batch/PCs), validation size (n=6,987), and similar effect sizes (OR per SD 1.311 vs 1.302). PGS001795 is an exact uterine-cancer label and uses a genome-wide shrinkage method (PRS-CS-auto; 911,692 variants) with large EUR evaluation (n=170,276), but it comes from a global biobank framework paper and provides only weak PRS-only R2 (0.001948) with no PGS-only AUROC; this makes it less clearly best-supported than the coherent uterine-neoplasm PRSweb models despite the larger validation set.

Main limitation: all candidates largely lack clean PRS-only AUROC, and several strong endometrial-carcinoma subtype scores exist (dominant subtype) that could be competitive; without a directly comparable PRS-only metric across uterine vs endometrial endpoints, confidence is not high."}

{"outcome":"DIRECT_SUB_OPTIMAL","best_model_id":"PGS002768","confidence":"Moderate","rationale":"All 13 candidates are direct matches to osteoporosis (trait_efo includes osteoporosis; phenotyping_reported is osteoporosis or closely related ICD10 M81 without pathological fracture). Several are from pan-trait/portability framework papers (e.g., PGS001273/PGS001274 from the “across 813 traits” UKB framework; PGS001883/PGS002095 from the “Portability of 245 polygenic scores” framework), which the provided domain knowledge flags as generally underperforming disease-focused models when alternatives exist.

Among the remaining non-framework-style options, PGS002768 (PRS-CS; osteoporosis; EUR GWAS and EUR evaluation) provides the clearest PRS-relevant discrimination evidence via an explicit per-SD effect size (OR=1.31, 95% CI 1.25–1.38) in a reasonably sized evaluation (n=39,444) with only basic covariates. Competing 2024 Commun Biol models (PGS004495/PGS004565 etc.) report ORs but are weaker (≈1.20–1.33) and have “Unknown” covariates, and the AllofUs 2024 models (PGS004809/PGS004810) report only incremental R2 rather than PRS-only metrics/effect sizes.

Limitation: no candidate here shows strong, directly comparable PRS-only AUROC/R2 across a disease-focused multi-cohort setting; most newer candidates lack PRS-only metrics, and the UKB framework models’ high full-model AUROC is explicitly discounted by the domain guidance. This keeps the recommendation below high confidence."}

{"outcome":"DIRECT_SUB_OPTIMAL","best_model_id":"PGS005262","confidence":"Moderate","rationale":"All 7 candidates are direct matches to the target concept of nodular goiter: two are for 'Nontoxic multinodular goiter' (PGS001814, PGS002022) and three are explicitly 'Benign nodular goiter' (PGS005261–PGS005263). Two additional models (PGS005273–PGS005274) are case-control contrasts 'thyroid carcinoma vs benign nodular goiter' and therefore are less endpoint-faithful for generic nodular goiter risk despite containing the target term.

Among the most endpoint-aligned options, PGS005262 is best supported by the visible evidence: it targets 'Benign nodular goiter' with matching trait_efo, is developed in a disease-focused global multi-ancestry thyroid study (multi-biobank cohorts listed), uses a genome-wide shrinkage method (PRSCS) with very high variant count (1,085,173), and shows the highest reported full-model AUROC within the benign nodular goiter set (0.5933) along with a moderate-to-strong per-SD effect size (OR 1.408). 

Main limitation: none of the candidates provide clearly PRS-only/comparable discrimination metrics (the UKB portability models report only partial-r ~0.024–0.028; the 2026 thyroid study models report full-model AUROC with 'covariates: Unknown'), so performance comparability is limited and selection cannot be made with high confidence. Additionally, the nontoxic multinodular goiter models are from a known UKB portability framework context, which is a negative signal relative to the disease-focused multi-cohort thyroid study but does not fully resolve uncertainty without cleaner PRS-only metrics."}
